TO CREATE MY SSH-KEY
*ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your_email@example.com" -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github 
# -t ed25519    -- Sets the key type/algorithm to Ed25519—a modern, fast, secure algorithm recommended for new keys. docs.github 
# -C "your_email@example.com"  --Adds a comment to the public key, usually your email. It is only a human-readable label; it does not affect security or authentication. docs.github 
#  -f ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github --Sets the output file path/name for the private key. ~/.ssh stores all ssh keys and this particular one is under id_ed25519_github 
#  ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github.pub --is automatically created alongside it; this is the public key you add to be added to the public. 
   # cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github.pub to access it, usually a one lined code
# (optional) -N ""	New passphrase, empty = no passphrase. Use only for scripts/CI where no prompt is possible. On a personal laptop, omit it and type a real passphrase at the prompt.
NOTE: This process will ask for a passphrase i.e a password, its always a good idea to put one

*eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"   
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github  #this adds the ssh key to the key agent, without it, it sits in ~/.ssh, but your computer doesn't automatically know to use it for your destination.        
# eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"	ssh-agent -s prints shell commands that start the agent and export SSH_AUTH_SOCK / SSH_AGENT_PID. 
  eval runs those exports in your current shell, so the agent is visible to later commands. Without eval, the agent starts but your shell can't find it.
# ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github	Loads the private key into the agent (asks for the passphrase once). The agent then signs for you until the session ends.
(optional) ssh-add -l	List keys currently loaded in the agent.
(optional) ssh-add -D	Delete all keys from the agent.

*ssh -T git@github.com # Testing the connection
# ssh	The SSH client.
-T	Disable pseudo-terminal allocation. GitHub doesn't offer shell access, so asking for a TTY produces a warning; -T suppresses it.
-v	Verbose — prints each step (which keys are offered, which are accepted). The most useful debugging flag.
-vv / -vvv	Even more verbose (very noisy; -v is usually enough).
-i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github	Identity file — explicitly tell SSH which private key to use. Essential when your key has a non-default name.
-p 443	Port — GitHub also listens on 443; useful when port 22 is blocked by a firewall: ssh -T -p 443 git@ssh.github.com.
-o IdentitiesOnly=yes	Option — tell SSH to use only the -i key, not try every key in the agent. Avoids "too many authentication failures".
git@github.com	User@host. git is GitHub's fixed SSH username (not your GitHub handle — always literally git).

SSH DOES WORK WITH WEAK PERMISSION
chmod 700 ~/.ssh  #7 = owner read+write+execute, 0 = group none, 0 = others none. Only you can enter the .ssh directory.
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github #Owner read+write only. Nobody else can read the private key. SSH will error if this is looser.
chmod 644 ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github.pub #Owner read+write, others read. Public key is safe to be readable.
Permission digit meaning: r=4, w=2, x=1, summed per group (owner/group/others).

##The config file (permanent alternative to ssh-add) 
CREATE => ~/.ssh/config and nano:
Host github.com
    HostName github.com
    User git
    IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github
    IdentitiesOnly yes
Line	What it does
Host github.com	The alias you type on the command line (ssh github.com). Can be any label.
HostName github.com	The real hostname SSH connects to.
User git	GitHub's fixed SSH user.
IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github	Which private key to use for this host.
IdentitiesOnly yes	Use only that key — don't spray all agent keys at the server.

With this file in place, git clone git@github.com:flavio/repo.git automatically picks the right key; no ssh-add needed per session
(though the agent still asks for the passphrase once per session unless you use a keychain).

FOR MULTIPLE CONFIG :
Use the Include directive in ~/.ssh/config to load multiple SSH config files. 
OpenSSH supports glob patterns, so you can automatically include every file in a folder such as ~/.ssh/config.d/.

Create a folder for split configs:
*mkdir -p ~/.ssh/config.d
Make your main config include them:
*nano ~/.ssh/config
Add:
Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*
Now every matching file in ~/.ssh/config.d/ is loaded as part of your SSH configuration. The * wildcard matches files in lexical (alphabetical) order.
~/.ssh/
├── config
├── config.d/
│   ├── 10-github.conf
│   ├── 20-work.conf
│   └── 30-servers.conf

~/.ssh/config:
Include ~/.ssh/config.d/*

~/.ssh/config.d/10-github.conf:
Host github.com
  User git
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_github
  IdentitiesOnly yes

~/.ssh/config.d/20-work.conf:
Host work-github
  HostName github.com
  User git
  IdentityFile ~/.ssh/id_ed25519_work
  IdentitiesOnly yes

Then you can connect with:
ssh -T git@github.com
ssh -T work-github




